"""King Arthur and the Round Table: a VenvLearning retelling for Grade 4. Chapters 1–6.
An original retelling of the public-domain Arthurian legends (Malory's Le Morte d'Arthur, 1485, and later retellings).
Chapter titles match the Grade 4 ELA lessons."""

CHAPTERS = []


def ch(title, words, paras):
    CHAPTERS.append({"title": title, "words": words, "paras": paras})


ch("The Birth of Arthur",
   [("prophecy", "a statement about what will happen in the future"), ("heir", "the person who will become king or queen next"),
    ("loyal", "faithful; standing by someone"), ("foster", "to raise a child who was born to someone else")],
   ["Long ago, before there were castles on every hill in Britain, the land was ruled by a king named Uther Pendragon. Uther was a strong warrior, but he was not a peaceful man. The great lords of Britain argued and fought among themselves, and the king spent most of his days at war.",
    "At Uther's side stood a man unlike any other in the kingdom. His name was Merlin. Merlin had a long white beard, eyes as sharp as a hawk's, and a voice that could hush a crowded hall. Some people said he was a wizard. Others said he was simply the wisest man alive. Everyone agreed on one thing: Merlin knew what would happen before it happened.",
    "Uther married a lady named Igraine, and before long they were going to have a child. One night Merlin came to the king.",
    "“My lord,” Merlin said, “I have seen what is coming. You will have a son, and he will become the greatest king this island has ever known. But danger is near. Your enemies are many, and your own life will not be long. When the child is born, you must give him to me. I will hide him and keep him safe until the time is right.”",
    "Uther did not want to give away his son. But he had learned that Merlin's words always came true. With a heavy heart, he agreed.",
    "On a cold winter night, the baby was born. Before the sun rose, Merlin was waiting at a small back gate of the castle. A servant placed the baby, wrapped in a cloth of gold, into his arms. Merlin pulled his cloak around the child and disappeared into the dark.",
    "Merlin carried the baby far away to the home of a good and honest knight named Sir Ector. Sir Ector had a son of his own, a boy named Kay. Merlin did not tell Sir Ector who the baby really was. He only said, “Raise this child as your own. Teach him to be brave, kind, and honest. One day you will understand why I have asked this of you.”",
    "Sir Ector was a loyal man. He promised to foster the baby, and he and his wife named the child Arthur. Arthur grew up in Sir Ector's house as Kay's younger brother. He did not know that he had been born a prince.",
    "Merlin's prophecy came true. Not long after Arthur was born, King Uther fell ill and died. Because no one knew that Uther had a son, there was no heir to take the throne. The great lords of Britain each wanted to be king, and they began to fight. For many years the land was full of trouble. Farms were burned, roads were unsafe, and ordinary people lived in fear.",
    "Through all those hard years, Arthur grew up quietly in the countryside. He learned to ride a horse, to hunt in the forest, and to care for weapons and armor. He served his older brother Kay as a squire, carrying his shield and helping him into his armor. Arthur was strong and quick, but he was also gentle. He was kind to the servants and the animals, and he always told the truth.",
    "Every so often, a tall old man with a long beard would pass by Sir Ector's land. He would stop and watch the boy at his work, and then he would go on his way without a word. Arthur did not know who the stranger was. But Merlin was keeping his promise. He was waiting for the right time.",
    "“Patience,” Merlin would say to himself as he walked away. “The boy is not ready yet. But the day is coming soon.”"])

ch("The Sword in the Stone",
   [("tournament", "a contest in which knights fought to show their skill"), ("anvil", "a heavy iron block used by blacksmiths"),
    ("archbishop", "a high leader of the church"), ("doubt", "to not believe; to be unsure")],
   ["When Arthur was about fifteen years old, Merlin went to the Archbishop of Canterbury, the most important leader of the church in Britain.",
    "“The land has been without a king for too long,” Merlin said. “Call all the lords and knights to London at Christmas. A sign will show them who the true king should be.”",
    "So the archbishop sent out the call. On Christmas morning, the lords and knights crowded into the great church in London. When the service ended and the people walked out into the churchyard, they gasped. In the middle of the yard stood a huge square stone. On top of the stone sat an iron anvil, and stuck through the anvil and deep into the stone was a shining sword. Golden letters were written on the stone:",
    "WHOEVER PULLS THIS SWORD FROM THIS STONE AND ANVIL IS THE TRUE-BORN KING OF ALL BRITAIN.",
    "One by one, the proudest lords stepped up. They grabbed the handle, braced their feet, and pulled with all their might. Their faces turned red. Their arms shook. But the sword did not move, not even the width of a hair.",
    "“The man who can pull this sword is not here yet,” said the archbishop. “But God will make him known.” He ordered that a great tournament be held on New Year's Day so that every knight in the land would come to London. Ten knights were chosen to guard the stone day and night.",
    "Sir Ector rode to London for the tournament with his son Kay, who had just been made a knight, and with Arthur, who was Kay's squire. As they rode toward the field, Kay suddenly pulled up his horse.",
    "“I lost my sword!” Kay cried. “I left it at the inn where we slept last night. Arthur, ride back and get it for me. Hurry!”",
    "Arthur turned his horse and galloped back to the inn. But the door was locked. Everyone had gone to watch the tournament. Arthur did not know what to do. “My brother cannot fight without a sword,” he thought. Then he remembered something he had seen as they rode through town: a sword stuck in a stone in a churchyard.",
    "Arthur rode to the churchyard. The guards had left their post to watch the tournament, so no one was there. Arthur jumped down, took hold of the sword by its handle, and pulled. The sword slid out of the stone as easily as a spoon slides out of honey. Arthur did not stop to read the golden letters. He jumped back on his horse and rode to Kay.",
    "When Kay saw the sword, his eyes grew wide. He knew at once where it had come from. He hurried to his father. “Father,” Kay said, “look! Here is the sword from the stone. I must be the king!”",
    "Sir Ector looked at his son for a long moment. Then he took both boys back to the churchyard and into the church. “Kay,” he said quietly, “tell me the truth. How did you get this sword?”",
    "Kay looked at the floor. “Arthur brought it to me,” he admitted.",
    "Sir Ector turned to Arthur. “And how did you get it?” Arthur told him exactly what had happened. Sir Ector led him back to the stone, and Arthur slid the sword back in. Then Sir Ector tried to pull it out, and so did Kay. They could not move it. Arthur took hold of the handle again, and once more the sword came out easily.",
    "Sir Ector fell to his knees, and Kay knelt beside him. “Father, brother, why do you kneel to me?” Arthur cried.",
    "“Because I am not your father,” said Sir Ector gently. “I see now why Merlin brought you to me. You are the son of King Uther Pendragon, and you are our king.” Arthur began to cry, because he loved Sir Ector and did not want to lose his family. Sir Ector smiled through his own tears. “I will always be loyal to you, my lord,” he said. “I ask only one thing. Make your brother Kay the steward of your lands.” Arthur promised at once.",
    "When the lords heard the news, many of them were angry. “A boy? A squire nobody has ever heard of?” they grumbled. They doubted that Arthur could be the true king. So the sword was put back into the stone, and the test was held again at Candlemas, and again at Easter, and again at Pentecost. Every time, the great lords failed, and every time, Arthur drew out the sword.",
    "At last the ordinary people of Britain shouted, “We will have Arthur as our king! No one else! It is God's will!” Then even the proud lords knelt before him. Arthur was crowned king, and he promised to rule fairly and to protect everyone in the land, rich and poor alike."])

ch("The Sword in the Lake",
   [("scabbard", "a case that holds a sword"), ("shimmering", "shining with a soft, wavering light"),
    ("mist", "a thin cloud of tiny water drops near the ground"), ("enchantment", "magic; a magic spell")],
   ["Being king was not easy. Some of the lords who had knelt before Arthur rose up against him, and the young king had to fight to keep the peace. Arthur was brave in battle, and with Merlin's advice, he won. But one day he met a knight who was stronger than he expected.",
    "The knight was named Sir Pellinore, and he guarded a road through the forest. He fought every knight who passed. Arthur rode up to challenge him, and the two knights crashed together. They fought for a long time until, with a terrible crack, Arthur's sword broke in two.",
    "Merlin arrived just in time to save the king. As they rode away, Arthur looked at the broken sword in his hand. “Now I have no sword at all,” he said sadly.",
    "“Do not worry,” said Merlin. “Not far from here there is a sword that is meant for you. Come with me.”",
    "They rode until they came to a wide, still lake. The water was so smooth that it looked like a sheet of silver glass. A soft white mist floated over its surface, and the only sound was the gentle lapping of tiny waves against the shore. Arthur caught his breath. In the middle of the lake, an arm dressed in shimmering white silk rose out of the water. In its hand it held a sword. The sword's handle sparkled with jewels, and its blade shone brightly, as if it had captured the light of the sun.",
    "“That is the sword I spoke of,” said Merlin. “Look! Here comes the Lady of the Lake.”",
    "A lady was walking across the water toward them. Her gown was the color of the sea, and her long hair moved as though a breeze were blowing through it, though the air was perfectly still. Merlin explained that she lived in a beautiful palace of rock beneath the water and that her enchantments were very old and very strong.",
    "Arthur bowed politely. “Lady,” he said, “what sword is that which the arm holds above the water? I wish it were mine, for I have no sword.”",
    "“Sir King,” said the Lady of the Lake, “that sword is mine. Its name is Excalibur. If you will promise to give me a gift when I ask for it, you may have it.”",
    "“I promise,” said Arthur. “I will give you whatever gift you ask.”",
    "“Then take the boat you see there,” she said, “and row out to the sword. Take it and its scabbard.”",
    "Arthur and Merlin climbed into a small boat that was waiting at the shore. Arthur rowed out across the misty water until he reached the arm. He took hold of the sword and the scabbard, and the hand let go and sank quietly beneath the surface.",
    "Back on shore, Arthur drew Excalibur from its scabbard. The blade gleamed. It was perfectly balanced and so sharp that it seemed it could cut through anything.",
    "As they rode away, Merlin asked, “Which do you like better, the sword or the scabbard?”",
    "“The sword, of course,” said Arthur.",
    "“Then you are not as wise as I hoped,” said Merlin. “The scabbard is worth ten of the swords. As long as you wear it, you will not lose blood, no matter how badly you are wounded. Keep the scabbard close to you always.”",
    "Arthur thought about Merlin's words. He had learned something important: sometimes the plain thing that protects you is worth more than the shining thing that everyone admires.",
    "From that day on, Arthur carried Excalibur wherever he went. With it he won many battles, and the people of Britain began to feel safe at last."])

ch("Sir Balin and the Hallows",
   [("hallows", "holy treasures"), ("misfortune", "bad luck"), ("rash", "acting too quickly without thinking"),
    ("wasteland", "land where nothing grows")],
   ["One day a lady came to King Arthur's court wearing a heavy sword belted around her waist. She explained that the sword had been put on her by magic and that she could not take it off. “Only a good and true knight, without any wickedness in him, can draw this sword,” she said. “Whoever draws it will set me free.”",
    "King Arthur tried first, and then all of his knights, one after another. None of them could draw the sword.",
    "Standing quietly at the back of the hall was a poor knight named Sir Balin. He had just been let out of prison, and his clothes were shabby. When everyone else had failed, he stepped forward. “Lady,” he said, “may I try?” The lady looked at his worn clothes and almost said no. But Balin took hold of the sword, and it slid out easily.",
    "Everyone was amazed. The lady thanked him and asked for the sword back. But Balin wanted to keep it. “Then you will be sorry,” the lady warned. “With that sword you will strike the person you love most in the world, and it will be your ruin.” Balin did not listen. He kept the sword, and from then on people called him the Knight with Two Swords.",
    "Balin was brave, but he was also rash. He acted before he thought, and misfortune followed him wherever he went. In a quarrel at the king's court, he struck down a guest, and King Arthur sent him away in disgrace. Balin rode out into the world, hoping to win back the king's favor with great deeds.",
    "His travels led him at last to the castle of King Pellam. This was no ordinary castle. Deep inside it were kept the hallows, the holiest treasures in all the land. Among them was a spear with a point that dripped with blood. People said it was the spear that had pierced the side of Christ.",
    "At King Pellam's feast, Balin got into a fight. He drew his sword, but it broke. King Pellam chased him through the castle, room after room. Balin was looking for any weapon at all. At last he burst into a beautiful chamber filled with gold and silver. On a table lay the strange spear. Without stopping to think, Balin grabbed it and struck King Pellam with it.",
    "This blow became known as the Dolorous Stroke, which means the painful, sorrowful stroke. As soon as the spear struck, the whole castle shook. Walls cracked and crashed down. Balin and King Pellam lay buried under the stones for three days.",
    "Merlin came and pulled Balin from the ruins. He told him what he had done. “Because of your blow,” Merlin said, “King Pellam will suffer from his wound for many years, and it will not heal. And his land will suffer with him.” When Balin rode away, he saw that Merlin was right. Fields had dried up. Trees had no leaves. People lay sick in the villages, and they cried out, “Balin, you have caused all this!” The land had become a wasteland.",
    "Balin rode on, sad and ashamed. At last he came to an island where a custom said that every visiting knight must fight the knight who guarded it. Someone gave Balin a different shield, one without his own coat of arms. He crossed to the island and found a knight in red armor waiting for him.",
    "The two knights fought fiercely for hours. At last both of them fell to the ground, badly wounded. Then the red knight took off his helmet, and Balin saw his face. It was his own brother, Balan, whom he loved more than anyone in the world. Because Balin had carried a strange shield, his brother had not known him.",
    "The lady's warning had come true. The two brothers forgave each other, and they died side by side. They were buried in a single grave.",
    "Merlin wrote their names on the tomb. He said that one day a better knight would come, a knight who would heal King Pellam and bring life back to the wasteland. That knight had not yet been born."])

ch("The Marriage of Arthur and the Founding of the Round Table",
   [("courteous", "polite and respectful"), ("chivalry", "the code of honor that knights promised to follow"),
    ("oath", "a serious promise"), ("perilous", "dangerous")],
   ["After a few years, King Arthur's lords told him that he should marry. Arthur agreed, and he already knew whom he wanted to marry. He had met Guenevere, the daughter of King Leodegrance of Cameliard, and he thought she was the most beautiful and courteous lady he had ever seen.",
    "Merlin was worried. He could see things that others could not. “She is fair and good,” Merlin said, “but I see sorrow coming if you marry her.” Arthur loved Guenevere, and he would not change his mind. Merlin sighed and went to Cameliard to ask King Leodegrance for his daughter's hand.",
    "King Leodegrance was overjoyed. “I will send King Arthur a gift that will please him more than gold or land,” he said. “I have a table that once belonged to his father, King Uther. It is round, and it can seat one hundred and fifty knights. I will send it to him, along with one hundred of my own knights to sit at it.”",
    "Guenevere traveled to Camelot, Arthur's city, with the great table and the hundred knights. Camelot was full of joy. There were flags on every tower, music in every hall, and flowers on every road. Arthur and Guenevere were married in the church of Saint Stephen, and afterward there was a feast that lasted for days.",
    "Then Merlin brought everyone into the great hall where the Round Table stood. “Why is it round?” someone asked. Merlin explained. “At a long table, the most important person sits at the head, and the least important sits at the far end. At a round table, there is no head and no foot. Every knight who sits here is equal to every other knight.”",
    "King Arthur nodded. He wanted his knights to be equal. He wanted them to be like brothers. Merlin chose the best knights in the land to fill the seats. When the knights sat down, the most wonderful thing happened. On the back of each chair, golden letters appeared, spelling out the name of the knight who belonged there.",
    "But one seat had no name. It stayed empty. Merlin called it the Siege Perilous, which means the Dangerous Seat. “No one may sit in this chair,” Merlin warned, “except the knight for whom it is made. Anyone else who tries will be destroyed. That knight will be the best knight in the world, and he has not yet come.” And so there was an empty seat at the Round Table, waiting.",
    "Then King Arthur stood and gave his knights a set of rules to live by. Every year at the feast of Pentecost, the knights promised again to keep this oath:",
    "They would never be cruel or murder anyone. They would never betray anyone or break their word. They would show mercy to anyone who asked for it. They would always help ladies, widows, children, and all people who could not protect themselves. They would never fight in a wrong cause, for money, or for selfish reasons. And they would stay loyal to King Arthur.",
    "This way of living was called chivalry. It was not easy. A knight of the Round Table had to be brave in battle, but he also had to be gentle, honest, and fair.",
    "From then on, knights from all over the world wanted to join the Round Table. People who were in trouble came to Camelot asking for help, and King Arthur always sent a knight to aid them. For the first time in many years, the land had peace and justice. It was the beginning of a golden age."])

ch("Sir Lancelot",
   [("renowned", "famous and admired"), ("enchantress", "a woman who uses magic"),
    ("captive", "a prisoner"), ("humble", "not proud or boastful")],
   ["Of all the knights who came to Camelot, the most famous was Sir Lancelot. Lancelot came from France. He was the son of King Ban of Benwick, but when he was a baby, his father died in a war and his mother lost him. The Lady of the Lake found the baby and carried him to her palace beneath the water. She raised him and taught him everything a knight should know. That is why he was called Lancelot du Lac, which means Lancelot of the Lake.",
    "When Lancelot was a young man, the Lady of the Lake brought him to Camelot, and King Arthur made him a knight. Soon Lancelot proved that he was the best knight of all. No one could beat him in a tournament. He was strong and quick, and he never gave up. But he was also humble and kind. He never bragged, and he always showed mercy to knights he defeated.",
    "Lancelot loved adventure. One hot summer day, he rode out with his cousin, Sir Lionel. Around noon, Lancelot grew sleepy, so he lay down to rest under an apple tree while Lionel kept watch.",
    "While Lancelot slept, a huge knight named Sir Turquine galloped past. Turquine hated the knights of the Round Table. He knocked Lionel off his horse, tied him up, and carried him away to his castle. In Turquine's dungeon there were already more than sixty prisoners, many of them knights of the Round Table.",
    "Meanwhile, four queens rode by and saw Lancelot sleeping under the tree. One of them was Morgan le Fay, an enchantress who was King Arthur's half-sister and his enemy. Morgan cast a spell so that Lancelot would not wake, and the queens carried him to their castle.",
    "When Lancelot woke up, he was in a cold stone room. The four queens told him, “You must choose one of us to serve, or you will stay here until you die.” Lancelot refused. “I would rather die,” he said, “than be forced to serve anyone by trickery.”",
    "The next morning, a young lady brought Lancelot his breakfast. She was kind, and she felt sorry for him. “If you will help my father in a tournament,” she whispered, “I will help you escape.” Lancelot gladly agreed. That night she unlocked twelve doors and led him out of the castle. Lancelot kept his promise and helped her father win his tournament.",
    "Then Lancelot went looking for his cousin Lionel. He met a lady who told him about Sir Turquine and the prisoners in his dungeon. She led him to Turquine's castle. Outside the gate hung a copper basin. Lancelot struck it with his spear so hard that the bottom fell out.",
    "Sir Turquine came riding out, and the two knights fought. They fought for two hours. Their shields were split and their armor was dented. At last they stopped to rest, leaning on their swords.",
    "“You are the strongest knight I have ever met,” said Turquine. “I will free all my prisoners and be your friend, as long as you are not the one knight I hate most: Sir Lancelot.”",
    "“I am Sir Lancelot du Lac,” said Lancelot. He would not lie about his name, even to save himself.",
    "Turquine roared and attacked again, but in the end, Lancelot defeated him. Then Lancelot opened the dungeon and set every captive free, including his cousin Lionel and many knights of the Round Table.",
    "When the freed knights returned to Camelot, they told everyone what Lancelot had done. Lancelot became the most renowned knight in the land. King Arthur loved him like a brother, and Queen Guenevere honored him above all other knights. Lancelot, in return, promised to serve the king and queen with all his heart."])
